hvf_set_phys_mem() turned a section that is not page aligned into an
unmap, and hv_vm_unmap() refuses a range it never mapped with
HV_BAD_ARGUMENT, which assert_hvf_ok() turns into an abort. Such a
section is never mapped, so there is nothing to undo. tpm-tis-device's
tpm-ppi RAM region is 1 KiB, under Apple Silicon's 16 KiB page, so every
virt machine with a TPM aborted under HVF at start (Windows 11 on Arm,
2ksbox tracks M20, M21, M23). QEMU 9.2 kept a slot list and unmapped
only what it had mapped. Accesses to the section trap, as before.

diff --git a/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c b/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c
index 8bd9154..2fcf625 100644
--- a/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c
+++ b/accel/hvf/hvf-all.c
@@ -113,8 +113,12 @@ static void hvf_set_phys_mem(MemoryRegionSection *section, bool add)
 
     if (!QEMU_IS_ALIGNED(size, page_size) ||
         !QEMU_IS_ALIGNED(gpa, page_size)) {
-        /* Not page aligned, so we can not map as RAM */
-        add = false;
+        /*
+         * Not page aligned, so it was never mapped as RAM, and HVF
+         * refuses to unmap it (HV_BAD_ARGUMENT): a 1 KiB tpm-ppi region
+         * aborted every boot with a tpm-tis-device. Accesses trap.
+         */
+        return;
     }
 
     if (!add) {
